                <div data-article-body="true" data-track-component="article body" class="c-article-body">

                    
                    <section aria-labelledby="Abs1" data-title="Abstract" lang="en"><div class="c-article-section" id="Abs1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Abs1">Abstract</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Abs1-content"><p>The increasing use of europium (Eu), a critical rare-earth element (REE), in light-emitting diodes, display technologies, phosphors, nuclear reactors, bioimaging, and anti-counterfeiting applications has led to increased production, consumption, and environmental release. Consequently, Eu(III) contamination has emerged as an environmental concern, particularly in wastewater from the mining, metallurgical, electronics, pharmaceutical, and nuclear industries. Although stable europium generally exhibits limited toxicity, significant human health and environmental risks are associated with radioactive isotopes such as <sup>152</sup>Eu and <sup>154</sup>Eu, which are released during nuclear activities. In addition, the persistence, mobility, bioaccumulation potential, and interactions of Eu(III) with aquatic systems may affect microbial communities, nutrient cycling, aquatic organism development, and the overall water quality. This review critically examines the sources, environmental fate, toxicological effects, and treatment technologies of europium-contaminated wastewater. Particular attention is given to advanced removal and recovery methods, including adsorption, membrane filtration, precipitation, solvent extraction, ion exchange, electrochemical processes, and photocatalytic treatment. Recent developments in composite adsorbents, nanostructured materials, metal–organic frameworks, functionalized biochars, and hybrid systems have been evaluated for removal performance, selectivity, regeneration, scalability, and cost-effectiveness. Technological innovations, resource recovery strategies, circular economy approaches, key challenges, research gaps, and future directions for sustainable Eu management are also discussed.</p></div></div></section>

                    

                    
    


                    
                        
                            
